[THOUGHT] The proposed diagnosis is “Right acetabular labral tear.” While this is plausible—given the mechanism (rear-end MVC with flexed knees, force transmitted through the hip), isolated severe right groin pain, and negative radiographs—it remains *unconfirmed* and *not yet supported by definitive imaging*. The MRI of the right hip is explicitly listed as *pending*, and no MRI findings have been reported or interpreted. Furthermore, key clinical data needed to strengthen this diagnosis are missing:  
- No assessment of hip-specific physical exam maneuvers (e.g., FABER, log roll, anterior hip impingement test);  
- No documentation of right hip range of motion, especially internal rotation or flexion-adduction-internal rotation (FAIR) provocation;  
- Functional weight-bearing status on the right leg is unknown — critical for distinguishing intra-articular pathology (often non–weight-bearing tolerant) from muscular strain;  
- Neurovascular exam of the right lower extremity remains unreported, though stated as “no neurologic symptoms”; however, subtle deficits (e.g., femoral nerve stretch signs, L2–L4 dermatomal assessment) could refine localization.  

Crucially, bilateral anterior knee pain is unexplained by an isolated right labral tear — suggesting either a broader injury pattern (e.g., dashboard impact causing simultaneous patellofemoral and hip trauma) or a competing/alternative diagnosis (e.g., bilateral MPFL injuries, occult patellar dislocations). Without MRI confirmation *and* without ruling out more immediately consequential entities (e.g., occult femoral neck stress injury or chondral delamination), the diagnosis is premature. With only 2 turns remaining (8/10), delaying decision until MRI is not feasible — but current evidence does *not* meet diagnostic sufficiency standards for a definitive label.

[DECISION] INCOMPLETE

[FEEDBACK] Required before final diagnosis:  
- MRI report of right hip (specifically assessing labral morphology, paralabral cysts, and bone marrow edema in acetabulum/femoral head/neck);  
- Documentation of right hip physical exam: FABER test, log roll, FAIR test, and passive internal rotation range of motion;  
- Clarification of right lower extremity weight-bearing status (e.g., “non–weight-bearing,” “toe-touch,” or “full weight-bearing”) and neurovascular exam (pulses, sensation in anterior thigh/femoral triangle, motor strength of hip flexion/knee extension);  
- Correlation of bilateral knee tenderness with MRI findings (e.g., MPFL discontinuity, patellar tilt/subluxation, or osteochondral lesion) to determine if knee pathology contributes to or confounds the clinical picture.

